response.text:
Assuming reset initializes the design, the following single lemma is sufficient. It combines a bound on the counter with a relation between the counter and the saved value; this conjunction is 1-inductive and implies `prop`.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((x <= n) && ((m == '0) || (m < x))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset establishes x = m = 0, so both conjuncts hold for every unsigned n. IDLE reestablishes them. In LOOP, x increments only when x < n, which prevents overflow and preserves x <= n. If unknown is true, m receives the old x and is therefore strictly less than the incremented x. Otherwise, m is unchanged, preserving either m = 0 or m < x. Exiting LOOP and remaining in DONE preserve both values. Thus the lemma is preserved by one transition. When n > 0, m = 0 implies m < n; otherwise, m < x together with x <= n implies m < n. When n = 0, the target's n <= 0 disjunct holds. Consequently, the lemma implies prop in every state, and their conjunction is 1-inductive."
    }
  ]
}
</json>
